# Byte-identical checkouts on every platform.
#
# This repository measures its own checked-out bytes in several places: the
# Wycheproof corpus is SHA-256-pinned per file, the key-format corpus is
# checked against its source RFCs, the reproducible-build gate compares
# artefact digests, and the note-artifact detector's calibration samples a
# fixed 8 KiB window of each tracked file.  Every one of those is a function
# of the exact bytes on disk.
#
# Git's default on Windows (``core.autocrlf=true``) rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout, so the working tree stops matching the committed blob: digests
# change, and a byte-offset window over a file larger than the window slides
# by one octet per preceding line.  That is not hypothetical — it failed all
# ten Windows CI jobs, because a 38 KiB document's 8 KiB head/tail sample no
# longer contained the same text it does on Linux and macOS.
#
# ``-text`` for everything disables both the clean and the smudge filter, so
# the working tree is the committed blob on every platform, and neither the
# gates nor a contributor's local run depends on how their git is configured.
# Line endings become a property of what is committed, which
# ``tools/check_line_endings.py`` then enforces directly.
* -text

# Vendored Wycheproof corpus is hash-pinned by wycheproof_vectors/manifest.json
# (per-file SHA-256).  Covered by the blanket rule above; kept explicit so the
# pinning requirement stays visible at the paths it applies to.
/wycheproof_vectors/vectors/*.json -text
/wycheproof_vectors/manifest.json -text

# Fuzzer seed corpora are opaque octets chosen to reach particular code paths.
# Two of them legitimately contain CRLF as *data*.  ``binary`` additionally
# suppresses textual diff and merge handling, which is meaningless for them
# and would present an octet change as a line change.
/fuzz/seed_corpus/** binary
